  "notes": "Mid-90s explicit funk-R&B with G-funk and West-coast-adjacent programming, 1995–1997. The research gives \"70–97 straight (backbeat 2&4)\" with four sourced tempos, so the mode sits at 92. Three drum deltas are quoted: \"808 kick layered with a 909\" and \"backbeat on 2&4 with no swing\" — so `swing.amount` is 0.50 and `offGridMs` is 0, unusual against this genre's 0.62 — and \"32nd-note hat/clap rolls as the movement device\", which is the single loudest parameter here: a `32`/`64` roll vocabulary at 0.45 per bar with a velocity ramp, against the parent's 0.3 of plain 16ths. \"Saturated finger-snaps\" is a snap lane at −4 dB. This is one of the section's genuine 808 models: \"808 sub-bass tuned to the vamp root with slow portamento\" is the whole low end, so `drums.bass808` is authored with role `bassline` and there is deliberately **no** `bassline` block — on this schema the 808 and a bass part cannot both sound, and the research says the 808 is the bass. \"A G-funk synth-lead riff an octave above\" and the entry's \"high whining lead synth as the countermelody\" are the counter at +12 with a 0.5 density. Harmony is \"one- or two-chord minor vamps\", so the single-chord family leads and the triad share is high. ⚠ The research marks the key claim and the talkbox colour `[LOW-CONF]`, and it says the West-coast drum grammar is \"sourced at tutorial/genre level and applied by lane\" — the roll and velocity numbers are therefore lane inference, not name-specific fact. ⚠ All velocity, density and portamento numbers are INFERRED. ⚠ `session.keys` is INFERRED: the entry's Keys line gives \"minor-mode pentatonic vamps [LOW-CONF]\" and enumerates no tonic, so the values here and their weights are this author's reading of that line. ⚠ **`chords.harmonicRhythm` re-weighted (2026-08-17, harmony variety floor).** The entry gives the one-chord and the two-chord bed equal billing — \"one- or two-chord minor vamps, high whining lead synth as the countermelody\" — and the engine's `vamp` value is *one* chord held for the whole clip (`generators::chords`: \"One chord for the whole pattern\"), so a `vamp` weight of 6-of-8 made the two-chord half of that sentence almost unreachable. `vamp` keeps the top weight; the two-chord reading is now reachable at both rates. ⚠ The split between those two rates is **INFERRED** — the entry names the device, not its rate.",
